course: dictionary
lesson: 1263
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Burst is a good example. A balloon bursts when it breaks open because there is too much air inside. But when «Alfie burst into tears», nothing broke: he started crying suddenly.
explain: Ask yourself what the sentence is about. In «Red kangaroos can hop at over 50 kilometres per hour in bursts», burst means short, sudden amounts of hopping, not things breaking. The clues are the speed and the word in.
explain: Look at the words next to burst. «Burst open» and «burst a bubble» both involve something breaking, while «burst out laughing» and «a burst of energy» show something sudden. Read the whole phrase, then decide which meaning fits.
fact: Burst can be a verb or a noun: you can burst something, or you can give a burst.
fact: «Burst» means to break open suddenly because there is too much inside, like a balloon or a pipe.
fact: To burst into or out of a place means to go in or out very suddenly.
fact: A burst is a short, sudden amount of something, like «a burst of speed» or «a burst of noise».
fact: To be bursting means to be very full, or to want something very much, as in «bursting with pride».
exercise: «A can of soda left too long in the freezer can bulge and burst.» What does burst mean here? | Turn cold and hard | *Break open suddenly because there is too much inside | Run away very fast | Sing loudly for a long time
exercise: «At home Jake's little brother Alfie took one look at the bruise and burst into tears.» What does burst into tears mean? | Tear the tissues into small pieces | Go to bed early | *Start crying suddenly | Throw tears at a wall
exercise: «Some seeds are shot out when the pod bursts open, like peas.» What does bursts open mean? | Turns bright green | *Splits open suddenly | Grows very tall | Falls quietly to the ground
test: Mum says, «I am bursting with pride.» What does bursting mean here? | *Very full of pride | Quiet and shy | Empty and sad | Lost in a crowd
test: «The kangaroo hopped away in a burst of speed.» What is a burst in this sentence? | *A short, sudden amount of speed | A slow walk | A loud bang from a balloon | A bubble that pops
exam: Emma says burst always means something breaks. Is she right? | No, it always means slow and quiet | Yes, it always means something breaks | *No, burst can also mean a sudden start, like burst into laughter, or a short, sudden amount | Yes, but only when balloons are involved
